 ### Finite Tiling
-A variant of Infinite Tiling which produces a finite number of repetitions of a shape [S3, unofficial]. It takes an additional vector $\vec{l}$ as input, where $\vec{l}$ is the corner of the box used to limit the number of repetitions of the shape [S3, unofficial].
+A variant that produces a finite number of repetitions of a shape by limiting the tiling to a specific box [S4, unofficial]. It takes an additional vector $\vec{l}$ as input, representing the corner of the box used to limit the number of repetitions. The implementation typically involves checking if the folded coordinate $\mathbf{q}$ lies within the bounds defined by $\vec{l}$ and returning an infinite distance (or a large value) if it is outside, effectively clipping the infinite grid [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per ray step, identical to evaluating a single primitive [G]. The modulo operation is a constant-time arithmetic operation [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional data structures are required [G].
-- **Performance:** Enables rendering of infinite detail at essentially zero extra cost [S2, unofficial]. This allows for real-time frame rates even as scene complexity grows, provided other optimizations (bounding-volume early-outs, reduced step counts) are used [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Extremely efficient for creating complex, repeating structures and fractals. No mesh storage or LOD management required [G].
-- **Limitations:** Only supports periodic tiling. Cannot easily create non-repeating or irregular patterns without combining with other techniques [G]. Normal estimation at tile boundaries can be noisy due to derivative discontinuities [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample. The modulo operation is a constant-time arithmetic operation.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional memory is required beyond the input and output vectors.
+*   **Performance:** Enables rendering of fractals (e.g., Mandelbulbs, Menger sponges) and infinite scenes at real-time frame rates, as scene complexity does not grow with the number of visible instances [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Extremely efficient for periodic structures; no need for scene graphs or instance buffers.
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot represent non-periodic or sparse infinite structures without additional logic. Precision issues may arise at extreme coordinate magnitudes.
 
 ## Background
-The technique is foundational to ray-marched fractal rendering, enabling the creation of Mandelbulbs and Menger sponges [S2, unofficial]. It leverages the mathematical property of periodicity to reuse a single SDF definition across an infinite domain [S1, unofficial]. The method is widely used in demoscene productions and real-time ray marching demos due to its efficiency and visual impact [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The technique is foundational in ray marching and procedural geometry, popularized by demoscene artists and researchers like Inigo Quilez, who used it to create fractals such as the Menger sponge [S2, S3, unofficial]. It leverages the mathematical property of periodicity to reuse a single distance function, a concept central to procedural texture generation and implicit modeling.
 
